        "timestamp": 1776031000,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement Americas and the Caribbean World Americas Peru extends voting for president and Congress amid delays at polling sites The delays threatened to disenfranchise many Peruvians, muddying what was already a complex race among a record 36 presidential candidates 4 -MIN READ 4 -MIN Listen Associated Press Published: 5:56am, 13 Apr 2026 Peru’s nationwide elections suffered severe delays at some polling stations, especially in the capital Lima, muddying what was already a complex race among a record 36 presidential candidates. The election, which was supposed to help Peruvians reset their political course after years of turbulence, quickly descended into confusion as many voters queued up on Sunday morning only to find that ballots had not arrived. The ONPE, a national body overseeing the elections, initially reported that just 1 per cent of polling stations had experienced delayed openings because a private company contracted to deliver ballots failed to show up on time. Advertisement By early afternoon, however, another national electoral authority said that as many as 30 per cent of polling sites had failed to open by 1pm. An independent observer group put the number lower, at 14 per cent, before later saying that 94 per cent of nationwide voting sites had opened by 2pm. Advertisement Whatever the final percentage, the delays threatened to disenfranchise a significant number of Peruvians and kicked off a round of finger-pointing, with the ONPE vowing to sue the ballot distributor while voters and monitors demanded answers. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
